  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` didn't handle prices with thousands separators. For a payload like `\"price\":\"2,450.50\"`, the regex didn't match and returned `null`. The next line, `m[1]`, then threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which surfaced as a 500. All 37 `POST /checkout` 500s in `logs/app.log` have this error, and each one is preceded by a price in the `9,999.99` form. The intermittency is because only orders containing a price of 1,000 or more were affected.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- Amounts with comma grouping such as `2,450.50` and `1,000` are now parsed correctly.\n- Input that still doesn't match, such as `abc` or `12,34`, now throws the documented `Invalid amount: <input>` error. Before, it crashed with a `TypeError`.\n\n**Regression tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- One test covers comma-grouped prices, both directly and through `checkout`.\n- One test covers invalid input throwing a clear error.\n\n`npm test` passes with 6 of 6 tests. I didn't run the new tests against the old code, so I haven't seen them fail before the fix.\n\nAn invalid price still throws, so `POST /checkout` would still return a 500 for it. I didn't check `src/server.js`. A 400 response there would be a sensible follow-up.",
